    {
      "id": "6jrd73e",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Radius and General Facts about the Earth"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Earth's structure is broadly divided into three main concentric layers: Crust, Mantle, and Core.",
        "Information regarding the Earth's interior is derived from direct sources (volcanic materials, deep drilling) and indirect sources (seismic waves, density variations).",
        "The Crust consists of two distinct types: Continental Crust (composed mainly of SIAL - Silica & Aluminium) and Oceanic Crust (composed of SIMA - Silica & Magnesium).",
        "The Mantle contains critical functional regions, including the semi-molten Asthenosphere which enables tectonic movement.",
        "Transitions between internal layers are defined by key boundary zones known as seismic discontinuities."
      ],
      "id": "zbm9agv"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u1j5gbz",
      "content": "• Average radius of Earth: about 6371 km."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "evx2vrx",
      "content": "• Earth's interior temperature increases with depth (geothermal gradient)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "n55ya42",
      "content": "• Density also increases toward the center."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nsjkwvc",
      "content": "• The Earth is composed mainly of iron, oxygen, silicon, magnesium and nickel."
    },
    {
      "id": "svv1y3p",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Sources of Information about the Earth's Interior"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "87l1qkl",
      "content": "A. Direct Sources"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6u6n0dw",
      "content": "• Mining and deep drilling projects."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ybzj2qy",
      "content": "• Volcanic eruptions bring materials from inside the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "03m6fsy",
      "content": "B. Indirect Sources"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "t7xlvy7",
      "content": "• Seismic waves produced by earthquakes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cevtqoo",
      "content": "• Gravitational force studies."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nxzc8bt",
      "content": "• Magnetic field observations."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9ee9s59",
      "content": "• Meteorite composition which is similar to Earth's interior."
    },
    {
      "id": "5v6mg03",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Major Layers of the Earth"
    },
    {
