          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "qkvotj5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Crust"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l0jvi6d",
      "content": "• The crust is the outermost layer of the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "z7y8yq4",
      "content": "• It is very thin compared to other layers."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kny31oc",
      "content": "• Average thickness is about 35 km under continents."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "y38sthk",
      "content": "• Oceanic crust is thinner (5–10 km)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e87rboh",
      "content": "• Main elements are oxygen, silicon, aluminum, iron and calcium."
    },
    {
      "id": "866bc8n",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Crust"
    },
    {
      "id": "bmjrm5y",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Thickness</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Composition</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Continental Crust</p>",
            "<p>30–70 km</p>",
            "<p>Granite rocks rich in silica and alumina</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Oceanic Crust</p>",
            "<p>5–10 km</p>",
            "<p>Basalt rocks rich in silica and magnesium</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ruqj9kd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Mantle"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qqkogej",
      "content": "• The mantle lies below the crust and extends up to about 2900 km depth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gvshmjf",
      "content": "• It forms nearly 84% of Earth's volume."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Continental Crust (SIAL)",
        "Oceanic Crust (SIMA)"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Dominant Minerals",
          "Silica and Aluminium",
          "Silica and Magnesium"
        ],
        [
          "Average Thickness",
          "30 - 70 km",
          "5 - 10 km"
        ],
        [
          "Relative Density",
          "Lower (~2.7 g/cm³)",
          "Higher (~3.0 g/cm³)"
        ],
        [
          "Rock Type",
          "Predominantly Granitic",
          "Predominantly Basaltic"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "knvqy0b"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "826xkyp",
      "content": "• The upper part of the mantle with the crust forms the lithosphere."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5y2plqa",
      "content": "• Below it lies the asthenosphere which contains partially molten rocks."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a2hwc6l",
      "content": "• The mantle plays a major role in plate tectonic movements."
    },
    {
      "id": "j2slw21",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Important Zones in the Mantle"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a0db2g9",
      "content": "• Lithosphere – rigid outer layer including crust and upper mantle."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6tjl3tp",
      "content": "• Asthenosphere – semi-molten layer where tectonic plates float."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lxburpf",
      "content": "• Mesosphere – lower mantle with higher density."
    },
    {
      "id": "ainyvs6",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Core"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rlt0ovx",
      "content": "• The core is the innermost part of the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0as4k0q",
      "content": "• It extends from 2900 km to the center at 6371 km."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2g61xtu",
      "content": "• It is mainly composed of iron and nickel."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5e15nw9",
      "content": "• The core is responsible for generating Earth's magnetic field."
    },
    {
      "id": "pn8vu4x",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Core"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Memorize the order of seismic discontinuities from top to bottom: Conrad (Upper/Lower Crust), Mohorovičić (Crust/Mantle), Repetti (Upper/Lower Mantle), Gutenberg (Mantle/Outer Core), and Lehmann (Outer/Inner Core).",
        "Differentiate between SIAL (Silica + Aluminium) forming lighter continental crust and SIMA (Silica + Magnesium) forming denser oceanic crust.",
        "Understand that transverse seismic S-waves cannot pass through liquid, proving the Outer Core is in a liquid state."
      ],
      "id": "lvovwmw"
    },
    {
